					<div class="col-lg-8 article-content --font-size-20">
													<p><span class="dropcap">T</span>he Shajareh Tayyebeh School in Minab was clearly marked, in English and Farsi, on Google Maps. The Iranian school’s website, preserved on the Internet Archive, shows photos of children, mostly girls, in classrooms and at play. No one could have reasonably mistaken the building for a military site.</p>
<p>Nevertheless, on the morning of 28 February 2026, the United States and Israel declared war on Iran with ‘Operation Epic Fury’, and the school was among the sites struck by the surprise barrage of missiles and bombs. In typical American and Israeli fashion, the aggressors struck again a short time later in a ‘double-tap’, which killed rescue workers and other people arriving at the scene.</p>
<p>More than a hundred children – as well as scores of adults including parents, teachers, and the school’s principal – were blown to pieces, torn to shreds, burned to death, or crushed by falling concrete. Videos of the aftermath showed parents screaming and crying, bloodstained backpacks scattered around, and the arms and hands of dead children sticking out of the rubble.</p>
<p>‘The United States would not deliberately target a school,’ Secretary of State Marco Rubio said a few days later. ‘We,’ said Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth, weighing in, ‘never target civilian targets.’</p>
<p>Video evidence established that the volley of projectiles directed at the school and surrounding area included a subsonic Tomahawk cruise missile, manufactured by the Raytheon corporation and sold to the US government for $2.5 million apiece. Israel doesn’t have Tomahawks, and Minab was marked as a strike target on a US military map displayed during a Pentagon news conference. United States Central Command, the formation in charge of American warmaking in the Middle East, implicated itself further by releasing a video of the Navy launching Tomahawk missiles on the day the school was hit.</p>
<p>The schoolhouse was adjacent to a dusty, disused compound, mainly consisting of a pharmacy and medical clinic, supposedly run by the Iranian military. The larger attack damaged or destroyed all fourteen buildings in the compound affiliated with the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps, suggesting that the Shajareh Tayyebeh School may have been included in the ‘target set’ by mistake.</p>
<p>The target set in question was reportedly generated by the Maven Smart System, a software program designed to speed up the targeting process. Maven was developed by Palantir, the company founded – in part with seed money from the CIA – by Jeffrey Epstein’s ‘great friend’ Peter Thiel, and now run by an eccentric sociologist and lawyer named Alex Karp.</p>
<p>Maven enabled the US and Israel to strike over a thousand targets in the first twenty-four hours of their war against Iran. In the past, they might only have been able to strike a tenth as many sites with the same air force and naval assets in an equal amount of time.</p>
<p>Hours after the Americans bombed the elementary school in Minab, Palantir’s technology helped deliver another strike on a teenage girls’ volleyball team in Lamerd, southern Iran, while they were practicing in a dilapidated gymnasium. That attack was carried out using Lockheed Martin’s newly developed Precision Strike Missile, which costs up to $3.5 million and is designed to explode in the air and blast its victims with a deadly spray of tungsten pellets.</p>
<p>Other targets swept into the frenetically paced bombing spree included an Iranian wrestling camp, a bodybuilding and fitness federation, a tennis federation, a rowing club, and the Iranian Red Crescent Society’s Peace Building, as well as a sports stadium, public park, and synagogue in Tehran.</p>
<p>When news of war with Iran broke, Palantir’s stock price approached its zenith. Maven’s targeting errors, which mirrored the glitchy performance of consumer-facing AI bots, were ignored by market speculators. More war meant more profit for Palantir, whose CEO donated $1 million in 2024 to a Trump-aligned political slush fund.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span class="dropcap">T</span>he Soviet-born, German photographer Nikita Teryoshin has sought to capture the ‘back office’ of this secretive economy. ‘We are rarely afforded a glimpse behind the curtains of the global arms business,’ he notes.</p>
<p>Intending to ‘document arms fairs on every continent’, Teryoshin attended twenty different defense exhibitions, ordinarily closed to the public, between 2016 and 2026. Most were in countries within the sphere of US military hegemony, including Poland, South Korea, Germany, France, UAE, Spain, and Greece; but others took place in China, Russia, Vietnam, India, and Brazil. The ongoing project, published in his book <em>Nothing Personal</em>, makes plain the multinational character of the global defense industry and security elites of every stripe.</p>
<p>Teryoshin describes the atmosphere at the arms fairs he attended as ‘the complete opposite of a battlefield’. A weapons convention is like ‘an oversized playground for adults’, he writes, ‘with wine, fingerfood and shiny weapons’.</p>
<p>He continues: ‘Dead bodies here are mannequins or pixels on screens of a huge number of simulators. Bazookas and machine guns are plugged into flatscreens and war action is staged in an artificial environment in front of a tribune full of high-ranked guests, ministers, heads of states, generals and traders.’</p>
<p>Teryoshin’s use of flash in well-lit environments recalls the pervy vibe of VICE and American Apparel in the era of the millennial hipster, but here what is exposed in suggestively raw detail are middle-aged men buying and selling weapons systems in banally luxurious settings. The results are photos of an unnervingly sterile clarity.</p>
<p>The F-35 Joint Strike Fighter program will cost American taxpayers more than the whole Iraq War. The F-35, which is plagued by a host of technical problems, repeatedly appears in these sometimes dryly comical scenes. The positioning of dumpsters in the foreground of one photo, which depicts a Lockheed Martin billboard advertising the F-35 as ‘essential to allied airpower’, makes it look like the defective boondoggle of a stealth warplane is nosediving into a garbage can. In another, men in suits, looking awkward and uncomfortable in the midday sun, wait in line like children at a fairground to sit in the cockpit of a mock F-35.</p>
<p>At a convention for the Association of the United States Army, three officers in green dress uniforms sit on stools with virtual reality goggles strapped to their faces, experiencing ‘the future today’. Textron Systems, which makes the VR goggles, also sells geospatial analytics software to aid in targeting air strikes.</p>
<p>None of the people in these photos have eyes. Or rather, they have eyes but their faces are averted, blocked, occluded, or masked, so that you see at most one eye at a time. Teryoshin seems to be suggesting that these clean-shaven salesmen of death are in a sense blind. Blindness and blandness are among the major themes of these bleak corporate tableaux, which highlight the anonymous and impersonal nature of wars waged with advanced technology against weaker, poorer nations.</p>
<p>Other than a strip of parched grass in a parking lot, there is not one child, animal, or plant to be seen in the photographs selected here. In the full collection of photos from <em>Nothing Personal</em>, the near-complete absence of nature is striking. Besides a potted plant, some cut flowers, or a fringe of parched trees beyond an airstrip, the only greenery is the camouflage of military uniforms. The artificiality of the backgrounds is a reminder that war, and the preparations for it, are planned in clean and cordial air-conditioned spaces.</p>
<p>By contrast, children, drawings of animals, and plants abounded in the photos on the website of the Shajareh Tayyebeh School, whose name, a borrowing from Arabic and a scriptural reference, roughly means ‘the goodly tree’. One depicted a group of small girls with bows on their heads in front of a mural of a cartoon giraffe.</p>
<p>The United States military killed them. The next trading day, the stock price of the Tomahawk’s manufacturer, Raytheon, hit its highest level in the company’s hundred-year history.</p>
